#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SESSION_NOTES-S238-through-S236.md.verify.sh — the losslessness proof for this shard.
#
# Generated by Session 242 (the SEVENTH trim). Re-runnable, read-only, exits non-zero on failure.
#
#   bash <this>              prove the seventh trim was lossless AND landed where it was declared
#   bash <this> --self-test  prove this proof can FAIL (84 mutants; exit 2 if any survives)
#
# Descended from Session 239's SESSION_NOTES-S235-through-S232.md.verify.sh, itself descended from
# Session 235's, Session 231's, Session 228's, Session 224's and Session 222's — read those for the
# rationale behind L0-L3, L4, L5-L7, L8-L9, L10-L11 and L12. Shards are WRITE-ONCE: this proof pins
# ITS OWN cut key and does not re-prove the earlier shards' cuts. Run all seven.
#
# NO ASSERTION IS WEAKENED HERE, BUT THREE INHERITED LITERALS ARE SPLIT, and every earlier proof's
# flat "narrowing nothing" would have been false in this one. An adversarial review caught the
# wording before commit. What changed:
#   * L8's PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md required string stops at "(Session 242," — its tail, the span, is
#     L13/prose's now.
#   * L8 no longer requires README.md's shard repo-map line; L13 requires that line's span tail, and
#     L8/set still fails if the line disappears (the `.verify.sh` line beside it does not match
#     SHARD_NAME's negative lookahead — that is M46's whole point).
#   * L12's shard-banner literals were re-cut to begin AFTER the span.
# NET REACH IS UNCHANGED: every byte the sixth trim required is still required, by L8, L12, L13 or
# L8/set. It is required in two pieces, because a string two assertions read gives the newer one no
# mutant of its own — the fourth trim's finding, applied forward. A session grepping for one of the
# old whole strings will not find it; that is the cost, and it is recorded here rather than denied.
#
# The fifth trim narrowed L2/b3 to the banner and attached a condition to that narrowing: b3 is safe
# in the banner-only form ONLY while TRANSFORM is None and L1 is present and evaluated. Both hold at
# this cut (TRANSFORM is None below; L1 runs in check()), so the narrowed form is carried forward
# unchanged. A trim that ever needs a rebase must restore b3's records half in the same commit.
#
# ONE ASSERTION IS NEW HERE, and the hole it closes is one the lineage walked past six times.
# L12 also GAINS ONE ARM (L12/unique) — a strengthening, not a narrowing; see L13's uniqueness
# note below for the measurement that forced it.
#
#   L13 (NAME AND SPAN) — the shard's own FILENAME, and every sentence that states which sessions
#      moved, held against the record ids actually present in the two files.
#
#      THE HOLE WAS MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED. `docs/methodology/PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md` states the
#      rule that gives a shard its name — a non-first shard is `<STEM>-<NEWEST>-through-<OLDEST>.md`
#      — and seven trims have now obeyed it by hand. Nothing has ever parsed a shard's name. Walk
#      the assertions with a shard misnamed `SESSION_NOTES-S238-through-S235.md` while holding
#      Sessions 238 -> 236: L5/3 compares the ROUTING clause against the cut key and passes (the
#      clause says 236-238, which is what the cut archived); L5/4 opens the file that clause names
#      and reads the ids inside it and passes (it really does hold 236-238); L8/set compares name
#      SETS between files and passes (every file names the same wrong shard); L12 measures sizes and
#      never looks at a name. The filename is routing information a human acts on — it is the first
#      thing `git grep` prints — and it was the one piece of routing information no assertion read.
#
#      THE SAME HOLE COVERS EVERY NARRATIVE SENTENCE OF THE SPAN. The pointer block's "Archived
#      Sessions 238 -> 236" and "holds Sessions 242 -> 239 only", the banner's headline, README.md's
#      repo-map comment and PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md's list entry are literal text pinned by L2/b1,
#      L6 and L8/required against declarations the same author wrote in the same hour. That is
#      exactly the shape L12 closed for SIZES, one field over: L12 asks "is 583 the number of lines
#      you moved?" and nobody was asking "is 238 -> 236 the span you moved?".
#
#      TWO INDEPENDENT HALVES, the shape L11 and L12 both use. The NAME half derives the expected
#      filename from the record ids in the shard and holds the declared base name against it. The
#      PROSE half derives both spans — archived from the shard, retained from the live file — and
#      holds them against seven declared literals in five texts, in three separator forms, because
#      this project writes the span as "238 -> 236" in prose, "238->236" in README.md's ASCII repo
#      map and "238->236" without spaces in CLAUDE.md's shard list. Neither half can satisfy the
#      other: a correctly named shard can still be described by a stale sentence, and a correct
#      sentence does not make the file it points at correctly named.
#
#      A THIRD ARM COUNTS THIS ASSERTION'S OWN CLAIMS, for the reason SIZES["mutants"] exists. The
#      pointer block tells a reader that L13 holds "the filename and seven sentences"; a reader who
#      believes seven while the file declares three has been misled by exactly the mechanism L12 and
#      L13 exist to stop. L13/census holds that count word against len(SPAN_PROSE).
#
#      ITS LITERALS ARE DISJOINT FROM L12'S AND L8'S ON PURPOSE, and that cost real edits rather
#      than being noticed for free. L8's PROJECT_CONVENTIONS.md required string was TRUNCATED before
#      the span it used to contain, and L12's banner literals were re-cut to start after the span,
#      so that moving a span trips L13 alone and moving a count trips L12 alone. A string two
#      assertions read gives the newer one no mutant of its own — the fourth trim's finding, applied
#      forward instead of rediscovered.
#
# AN ASSERTION WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED HERE TOO, and the measurement is the whole reason.
# Every pointer block states how many shard BANNERS have gone stale, and nothing derives it. An
# L14 was drafted that would count the ancestor banners whose routing tables disagree with the live
# authority. It was dropped because IT WOULD HAVE MEASURED FOUR WHERE THE TRUTH IS FIVE: the S224,
# S227, S231 and S235 banners carry tables in the parseable `**a <= N <= b** -> file` clause form,
# but the S220 banner states its routing as PROSE ("the live ledger when N >= 221") and the S216
# banner states no forward-looking routing rule at all, in those words. Mechanising the parseable
# predicate would have turned a correct sentence red and taught the eighth trim to write a false
# number to satisfy a proof. The sixth trim rejected a prefix-relation L13 for the same class of
# reason. Recorded here so the eighth does not rediscover either and ship it.
#
# MEASURED COVERAGE — every line below was produced by running the loops, not by predicting them.
# Neuter one assertion at a time and re-run --self-test; what survives is what it ALONE catches:
#
#     for A in L0 L1 L2 L3 L4 L5 L6 L7 L8 L9 L10 L11 L12 L13; do
#       sed "s|^    fails += $A(.*|    pass|" <this> > /tmp/n.sh
#       echo "$A: $(bash /tmp/n.sh --self-test | grep SURVIVED)"; done
#
#     L0 (none)   L1 (none)*  L2 M13-M15,M40,M51   L3 (none)   L4 (none)   L5 M19-M26,M38,M39,
#     M47,M52   L6 M27   L7 M29,M30   L8 M31-M34,M41,M43-M46,M50   L9 M35-M37,M48   L10 M53-M55
#     L11 M56-M59,M84   L12 M60-M68,M82   L13 M69-M81,M83
#
#     * L1's blank entry is NOT a licence to remove it. L3 and L4 happen to catch every mutant L1
#       catches, so nothing depends on L1 ALONE — but L1 is the assertion the fifth trim's
#       narrowing of L2/b3 rests on, and b3's records half is dead only while L1 runs and
#       TRANSFORM is None. Read '(none)' here as 'overlapped', never as 'removable'.
#
#     Two entries MOVED relative to the sixth trim, and both moves are informative rather than
#     regressions. M18 was L5's alone there; here L2/b3 catches it too, because the pre-trim front
#     matter really does contain the line `**N >= 236** -> `SESSION_NOTES.md`.` that M18 writes into
#     the banner. M10 and M12 were L2's alone; here M10 also trips L12/unique (reverting
#     substitution 1 restores a second copy of a declared literal) and M12 also trips L11/target
#     (this cut lands at exactly 1,050, so restoring the longer stale paragraph pushes the live file
#     over the declared target). Nothing was weakened; more assertions arrived at the same mutants.
#     This is why the list is re-measured at every trim rather than copied.
#
# THAT LOOP IS NOT ENOUGH, and this lineage has proved it six times now: the SECOND trim shipped
# L2/b0 reachable by no mutant, the THIRD shipped L5/2's partition arm, an adversarial sweep of the
# FOURTH found four more, the SIXTH found two of its own, and at THIS trim the arm sweep found one
# (L13/census-absent) before commit and an adversarial review found three more the sweep could not
# see. An assertion is a handful of separate `if`s; neutering the whole function cannot tell you
# that one of those `if`s has never fired. So neuter one `if` at a time as well. All 55 arms between
# `def L0` and `def check` were swept that way here; 26 have a uniquely-catching mutant and 29 do
# not. The mutant each of those 26 arms alone catches:
#
#     L2/b3 M51             L5/1 M19-M23,M26      L5/2 loop M39        L5/4 stray M25
#     L7/differs M29        L8/required M32,M50   L8/forbidden M44     L8/set M45,M46
#     L9/differs M35,M37    L10/differs M55       L11/fire M56         L11/target M57
#     L11/floor M58         L11/declared M59      L11/figure M84       L12/numeric M60-M62,M68
#     L12/cap M63           L12/absent M65,M66    L12/unique M82       L12/figure M64,M67
#     L13/name M69          L13/absent M72,M74,M76                     L13/unique M83
#     L13/span M70,M71,M73,M78-M81                L13/census-absent M77
#     L13/census-word M75
#
# FOUR OF THIS TRIM'S ARMS FAILED THAT DISCIPLINE BEFORE COMMIT, and the fix was a mutant or a
# restructure every time, never a weakened assertion. One was found by the sweep; three were found
# only because an adversarial review reproduced them:
#   * L13/census-absent HAD NO MUTANT. Dropping the census sentence from the pointer alone is caught
#     by L2/b1 first. M77 drops it from the POINTER DECLARATION too, which satisfies b1 and leaves
#     the sentence genuinely missing — the only state in which this arm is the sole objector.
#   * L13/span WAS EXERCISED FOR 2 OF 8 DECLARED SENTENCES. M72/M74/M76 move a span in an
#     out-of-reach file and leave SPAN_PROSE's literal correct, so the DIVERGENCE arm fires and the
#     span arm never runs. M78-M80 move both. The first draft's comment claimed the opposite; the
#     review measured it.
#   * L12/absent AND L13/absent LOST THEIR MUTANTS TO THEIR OWN NEW SIBLINGS. Written as
#     `literal not in text` followed by `count != 1`, an absent literal has count 0, so neutering
#     the first arm let the second catch its mutants. Counting once and branching `n == 0` / `n > 1`
#     restored both. Two arms that catch each other's mutants are one arm wearing two names.
#   * L11/fire, L11/target AND L11/floor WOULD HAVE LOST THEIRS to the new L11/figure, because
#     M56-M58 moved an integer while leaving the rule sentence alone — which is exactly what
#     L11/figure objects to. They now move the sentence with the integer, which is also what a trim
#     genuinely relaxing the rule would do, and M84 relaxes the integer alone.
#
# TWENTY-NINE ARMS HAVE NO UNIQUELY-CATCHING MUTANT, and saying which — and why — is more useful
# than inventing contrived ones. This list is MEASURED; do not trust it after editing anything:
#
#   * L0/empty, L0/trailing, L13/empty-zone — no artifact mutation can reach them. Emptying a
#     records zone moves bytes, so L1, L3 and L4 fire first. They guard a GRAMMAR break, which is
#     not expressible as a mutation of the artifacts. Guard rails, and that is the honest name.
#   * L1's and L6's success-guard arms, and L7's NA arm — these are early returns ON SUCCESS, so
#     neutering them makes the assertion fire on EVERYTHING and no mutant can survive. The arm sweep
#     is structurally blind to that shape; the function-level loop above is what measures them, and
#     it gives L6 a unique mutant (M27).
#   * L2/b0 — M14, M15 and M40 fire it, but each also breaks b1 and b2, so none depends on it.
#     Kept anyway, because b0 NAMES the cause where b1 only shows bytes.
#   * L2/pointer, L2/b1, L2/b2 — mutually covering. M8 trips pointer and L5/1; M13-M15 and M40 trip
#     b1 and b2 together.
#   * L3/MISSING, L3/ADDED, L3/ORDER — subsumed by L1 and L4 on every mutation available here.
#   * L4/retained, L4/archived — subsumed by L5/4 since the third trim added it, as that trim said.
#     Kept: L4 states the cut key directly instead of inferring it, and names both sides on failure.
#   * L5/2 shape — the arm sweep CANNOT measure it: neutering that guard lets the gap/overlap loop
#     dereference an open bound and the run dies with a TypeError instead of surviving. Its coverage
#     is read off the PER-MUTANT ATTRIBUTION COLUMN that `--self-test` prints — run it and M47's row
#     ends `-> L5/2 shape`. (That column, not the arm table above; a reviewer read this sentence as
#     pointing at the table and reasonably called it dangling.) The same is true of every `is None`
#     and `is MISSING` arm in L7, L8, L9, L10, L12 and L13.
#   * L5/2 final junction — STRUCTURALLY subsumed: it is the junction between the shard clause and
#     the live clause, and L5/3 pins both exactly, so no single-clause mutation can reach it.
#   * L5/3 shard, L5/3 live — M24 also trips L5/4.
#   * L5/4 vacuous — mutually covering with L5/4 unreadable for a None result.
#   * L8/unreadable, L12/prose-unreadable and L13/prose-unreadable — MUTUALLY COVERING ACROSS THREE
#     assertions now, where the sixth trim had two, and that is WEAKER, not better. M49 makes
#     README.md unreadable and all three fire. It stays honest rather than contrived for the same
#     reason it did at the sixth trim: L13's nullable texts are a SUBSET of L8's four files, exactly
#     as L12's are — its other two, the live pointer and the shard banner, come from the artifacts
#     and can never be None. If a future trim gives L12 or L13 a text L8 does not read, that stops
#     being true and that arm needs its own mutant.
#
# The grammar is FROZEN into this file on purpose: a later change to the trim design must not
# silently change what THIS shard's proof means.
set -euo pipefail
exec python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
