if any(f.startswith("L1:") for f in fails):
    # The narrowing's whole justification is that L1 pins these bytes. On the one run where L1 is
    # RED that justification is void, and printing it as a fact would tell the reader the removed
    # check was redundant exactly when it was not.
    #
    # THE COLON IS A FIX, NOT A STYLE CHOICE, and it was found by this proof failing its own first
    # run. Inherited from the fifth and sixth trims, this test read `startswith("L1")` — which also
    # matches L10, L11, L12 and L13. Both ancestors ship assertions in that range, so on ANY L1x
    # failure they print "L1 IS RED ON THIS RUN" while L1 is green, telling a reader that the bytes
    # b3 stopped scanning are unpinned when they are pinned. L1's own message is the only one that
    # begins "L1:". The ancestors are NOT patched: they are write-once and L10 holds them against
    # their freeze commits, so correcting them there would turn this proof red. Reported instead.
    print("         *** L1 IS RED ON THIS RUN. These lines are NOT pinned, so the half the fifth")
    print("         *** trim narrowed away would have carried information here. Treat each as a")
